The following are some instances, not intended to be exhaustive, of when the Court could order a retrial on the ground of miscarriage of justice :
(a) The trial court has proceeded with the trial in the absence of jurisdiction;
(b) The trial has been vitiated by an illegality or irregularity based on a misconception of the nature of the proceedings; and (c) The prosecutor has been disabled or prevented from adducing evidence as regards the nature of the charge, resulting in the trial being rendered a farce, sham or charade."
